[gd_scene format=3 uid="uid://bl4n0r1mtr4y1"]

; THE TRAY IS AN OBJECT, NOT A ROOM. It has no camera, no light and no environment of its own, so
; opening this scene alone renders black - which is correct. Open res://table.tscn to see it.
;
; THE MODEL IS THE TRAY. models/tray/dice_tray_basic_rectangle_updated.glb (Kathleen's, 2026-10-01: the
; authored rectangle with its rim raised to twice the height) is one continuous mesh in two
; surfaces - Tray_Wood for the rim and Tray_Liner for the floor - and the .import binds those to
; tray_wood047.tres and felt_green.tres. It replaced five box primitives whose leaning walls left a
; visible gap where they met the floor box; a modelled tray has no seam to gap.
;
; THE MODEL IS ALSO THE SOURCE OF TRUTH FOR THE SHAPE. The collision below is fitted to it, not the
; other way round: the floor box is the liner quad and the four walls are the model's own inner
; faces. Re-fit them if the model changes - the numbers come from its geometry and nowhere else.
;
; TrayModel IS NOT PARENTED TO EITHER BODY, AND MUST NOT BE. DiceTray.ApplySkin dresses every
; MeshInstance3D under TrayFloor and TrayWalls with one material each, which would flatten this
; mesh's two surfaces to a single colour. So the skins now set the tray's PHYSICS and its AUDIO;
; its LOOK comes off the model's own materials. Swapping skins changes how the tray bounces and
; sounds, and no longer changes what it looks like - a skin that should look different needs a
; model, or a shader on this node.
;
; Scaled to 0.2145, the old 0.33 times the 0.65 footprint of 2026-10-02: the model is authored 1.6 x 2.0 units, and
; this puts the felt at 0.321 x 0.401 m with the rim 0.075 m above it - the size the code resize measured best, now
; in the model, uniform, with no stretched wood grain. THE SCALE IS ON THIS MESH ONLY, never on the tray node - see
; the warning in table.tscn about scaling rigid bodies.
;
; The floor is one box and the walls are four more, in TWO StaticBody3D: physics_material_override
; lives on the body, not on the shape, so a felt floor and wooden walls have to be two bodies or
; they would share one bounce. DiceTray measures all of it at _Ready, so moving a wall moves the
; tray's idea of where the felt ends without anything here being told twice.
;
; The walls are VERTICAL, because the model's are. The boxes they replaced leaned out by eight
; degrees to turn a climbing die back down rather than stop it dead; the modelled rim is straight.
; check-dice.ps1 is the thing that says whether a change here matters - run it after any change to this file.
;
; SMALLER, WITH TALLER WALLS (cc_task_ui-issues-10-01.md 6, 2026-10-02). Kathleen: "make the tray smaller so
; that when it's brought to you the dice are easier to see". It was first done in code (Footprint 0.65 and
; WallHeight 1.25 on the node, DiceTray.Sizing, which stretched the rim's wood); since 2026-10-03 it is the
; updated model at 0.2145, the collision below fitted to it, the release points and the dice's seats moved in by
; the footprint, and the throw softer by the root of it (die.tscn's ThrowSpeedMin/Max). Footprint and WallHeight
; are 1 again; they still resize whatever model is here, for trying a size (--tray-footprint, --tray-walls). The
; 2026-10-02 measurements of the code resize: Measured over 1000 throws of 2d20 and 250 of 4d6:
; at 1/1, 5 and 18 dice left the tray; at 0.65/0.65 (a plain shrink, walls 37 mm) 73 and 107; at 0.65/1
; 10 and 23; at 0.65/1.25, 1 and 10. All uniform. Eight d20s at once in it cocked on each other on 7% of
; dice (1.4% in the authored tray) and four on 1.2%, so MostAtOnce = 4: a Fireball's 8d6 goes up as two
; handfuls.
;
; EIGHT SEATS. A check is one d20, advantage is two, and damage is a handful - eight covers a
; Fireball's 8d6 in one throw. Anything larger (Meteor Swarm is 20d6) the tray throws in
; successive handfuls, the way a person does, and adds them up.
;
; TO LAY OUT: the throw points are guesses at where a hand lets go. They want an eye on them -
; dice that leave on the same arc land on top of each other, and that is where cocked landings
; come from. Points 4 and 5 moved in from x 0.24 to 0.19 because the new well is narrower and a
; die released at 0.24 would have been born inside the wall. Nudge them, throw a few hundred, and
; see.

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://Tray/DiceTray.cs" id="1_tray"]
[ext_resource type="PackedScene" uid="uid://bl4n0r1md1e01" path="res://Dice/die.tscn" id="2_die"]
[ext_resource type="Resource" path="res://Tray/skins/wood.tres" id="3_skin"]
[ext_resource type="PackedScene" path="res://models/tray/dice_tray_basic_rectangle_updated.glb" id="4_model"]

; the liner quad, to the millimetre: 1.4956 x 1.8694 model units at 0.2145
[sub_resource type="BoxShape3D" id="Shape_floor"]
size = Vector3(0.320806, 0.02, 0.400986)

; the model's inner faces at x = +-0.1604 and its outer at +-0.1716, so the box IS the wall, as tall as the rim
[sub_resource type="BoxShape3D" id="Shape_wall_x"]
size = Vector3(0.011197, 0.081596, 0.429)

; and the same across the ends. These run the full outer width, so they overlap the side walls in
; the corners rather than meeting them - a static body cannot gap against itself that way
[sub_resource type="BoxShape3D" id="Shape_wall_z"]
size = Vector3(0.3432, 0.081596, 0.014007)

[node name="DiceTray" type="Node3D"]
script = ExtResource("1_tray")
Skin = ExtResource("3_skin")
MostAtOnce = 4

; y = -0.038567 drops the model's underside onto y = 0, so the tray's origin is the face it
; stands on and table.tscn can set it down without knowing anything about the mesh
[node name="TrayModel" parent="." instance=ExtResource("4_model")]
transform = Transform3D(0.2145, 0, 0, 0, 0.2145, 0, 0, 0, 0.2145, 0, -0.038567, 0)

[node name="TrayFloor" type="StaticBody3D" parent="."]

; centre -0.002986 puts the top of the box on 0.007014, which is where the liner is. Dice rest on
; the felt they can see, not a millimetre above or below it
[node name="Floor" type="CollisionShape3D" parent="TrayFloor"]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, -0.002986, 0)
shape = SubResource("Shape_floor")

